'''Annie Elizabeth Andrews''' ({{nee|'''Lintzenich'''}}; born August 9, 1980) is an American pediatrician and politician who is the [[United States Senate|United States senator-elect]] from [[South Carolina]].<ref>{{Cite web |title= South Carolina U.S. Senate Election 2026 Live Results: Annie Andrews Wins |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-elections/south-carolina-senate-results |access-date= 2026-11-04 |website= www.nbcnews.com |language= en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last= Haberman |first= Maggie |last2= Epstein |first2= Reid J. |last3= Cohn |first3= Nate |last4= Oreskes |first4= Benjamin |last5= Goldmacher |first5= Shane |last6= Reynolds |first6= Evan |last7= Zhang |first7= Christine |last8= Haberman |first8= Maggie |last9= Soler |first9= Caroline |title= South Carolina U.S. Senate 2026: Live Midterm Election Results, Graham vs. Andrews |url= https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/11/03/us/elections/results-south-carolina-us-senate.html |access-date= 2026-11-04 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref> A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she defeated Republican incumbent [[Darline Graham]] in the [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2026 United States Senate election]],<ref name="Hubbard2026-1" /> becoming the first woman elected to the Senate from South Carolina and the first Democrat to win a Senate election in South Carolina since [[1998 United States Senate election in South Carolina|1998]].<ref name="GrisalesSchapitl2026-1" /> She will assume office on January 3, 2027.

Born in [[Paducah, Kentucky]] and raised in [[Indianapolis]], Andrews received her [[Doctor of Medicine]] from the [[University of Cincinnati]] and a master's degree in clinical research from [[Medical University of South Carolina]]. She worked as a pediatric hospitalist at [[MUSC Shawn Jenkins Children's Hospital]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robertson |first=Mike |date=2022-04-04 |title=Dr. Annie Andrews to Speak at College’s Bully Pulpit Series |url=https://today.cofc.edu/2022/04/04/dr-annie-andrews-to-speak-at-colleges-bully-pulpit-series/ |access-date=2026-11-09 |website=The College Today |language=en-US}}</ref> and was a professor of pediatrics at MUSC.<ref name="Brack2026" /> Her medical career included research and advocacy concerning firearm injuries among children.<ref>{{Cite web |title={{!}} Children’s National Continuing Education |url=https://ce.childrensnational.org/chai-affiliate-faculty-learning-lunches-2026/node/3973/bio/6083/view |access-date=2026-11-09 |website=ce.childrensnational.org}}</ref> Andrews was the Democratic nominee for [[South Carolina's 1st congressional district]] in the [[2022 United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina#District 1|2022 election]], losing to incumbent Representative [[Nancy Mace]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Carolina's 1st Congressional District election, 2022 |url=https://ballotpedia.org/South_Carolina%27s_1st_Congressional_District_election,_2022 |access-date=2026-11-09 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Annie Elizabeth Lintzenich was born on August 9, 1980<ref>{{Cite web |title=Annie Andrews |url=https://www.jacpac.org/candidates/annie-andrews |access-date=2026-11-04 |website=JAC |language=en}}</ref><ref name= "Brack2026" /> in [[Paducah, Kentucky]], and grew up in [[Indianapolis]], where she graduated from [[Cathedral High School (Indianapolis)|Cathedral High School]].<ref>{{Cite Facebook |postid=1828099248722001 |user=cathedralhighschool |title=While we have been celebrating Cathedral women trailblazers on Fridays, this news is simply too big not to share today! Congratulations to Dr. Annie (Lintzenich) Andrews '98 on winning the U.S. Senate race in South Carolina! |author=Cathedral High School |date=November 4, 2026}}</ref> She received her bachelor's degree from the [[University of Dayton]], where she was a member of [[Chi Omega]] sorority. She went on to earn her master's degree in clinical research from the [[Medical University of South Carolina]], and her Doctor of Medicine degree from the [[University of Cincinnati]]. She also completed a master's degree in clinical research after starting work at the [[MUSC Shawn Jenkins Children's Hospital]].<ref name="Batey2026">{{Cite news |last= Batey |first= Eve |date= July 12, 2026 |title= Who is Annie Andrews, the Democratic Contender for Lindsey Graham's Former Senate Seat? |url= https://www.vanityfair.com/story/annie-andrews-lindsey-graham-november-election |access-date= July 14, 2026 |work= [[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]] |language= en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260712210622/https://www.vanityfair.com/story/annie-andrews-lindsey-graham-november-election|archive-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

== Medical career ==
Andrews completed her [[medical residency]] in pediatrics in [[Cincinnati]], Ohio, before moving to South Carolina's [[Lowcountry]] in 2009. She worked for 14 years at [[MUSC Children's Hospital]] in [[Charleston, South Carolina|Charleston]],<ref>{{Cite news |last= Agrawal |first= Nina |date= April 18, 2026 |title= The Doctor Will Seek Your Vote Now |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/18/us/politics/democratic-doctors-running-for-congress.html |access-date= July 12, 2026 |work= [[The New York Times]] |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260419044922/https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/18/us/politics/democratic-doctors-running-for-congress.html |archive-date= April 19, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> as a pediatric [[hospitalist]].<ref>{{cite press release|date= Winter 2023 |title= Alumni Spotlight: Annie Andrews, MD |url= https://alumni.cincinnatichildrens.org/s/resources/winter-2023-newsletter/alumni-spotlight-annie-andrews |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240719183231/https://alumni.cincinnatichildrens.org/s/resources/winter-2023-newsletter/alumni-spotlight-annie-andrews |archive-date= July 19, 2024 |access-date= July 12, 2026 |publisher=[[Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center]] |language= en-US}}</ref> She announced in September 2022, while running for Congress, that she was taking an unpaid leave "to protect myself and my colleagues".<ref name= "Batey2026" /> She resigned in June 2023.<ref>{{Cite news |date= June 4, 2023 |title= Dr. Annie Andrews announces departure from MUSC |url= https://www.counton2.com/news/local-news/charleston-county-news/dr-annie-andrews-announces-departure-from-musc/ |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250531224450/https://www.counton2.com/news/local-news/charleston-county-news/dr-annie-andrews-announces-departure-from-musc/ |archive-date= May 31, 2025 |access-date= July 12, 2026 |work= [[WCBD-TV]]|language= en-US}}</ref>

As of mid-2025, Andrews worked part-time as a pediatrician in [[Children's National Hospital]] in [[Washington, D.C.]], but lived in Charleston.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Bustos |first= Joseph |date= May 29, 2025 |title= This SC pediatrician ran for Congress in 2022. Now she wants to oust Sen. Graham |url= https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article307292841.html |access-date= July 14, 2026 |website= [[The State (newspaper)|The State]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250530152820/https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article307292841.html|archive-date=May 30, 2025}}</ref>

== 2022 U.S. House of Representatives campaign ==
{{Main|2022 United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina#District 1|l1 = 2022 South Carolina's 1st congressional district election}}
[[File:South Carolina's 1st congressional district (since 2023).svg|thumb|[[South Carolina]]'s [[South Carolina's 1st congressional district|1st congressional district]] (since 2023)]]
Andrews became interested in political activism after the [[Parkland high school shooting]] in 2018, which convinced her to join the [[gun safety]] advocacy group [[Moms Demand Action]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last= Janssen |first= Emma |date= July 14, 2026 |title= She Wants to Win Lindsey Graham's Senate Seat—and Impeach RFK Jr. |url= https://newrepublic.com/article/213024/annie-andrews-south-carolina-senate-lindsey-graham-rfk |access-date= July 14, 2026 |magazine= The New Republic |issn= 0028-6583|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260720170632/https://newrepublic.com/post/213024/annie-andrews-south-carolina-senate-lindsey-graham-rfk|archive-date=July 20, 2026}}</ref> She began doing clinical research into [[gun violence]] prevention to reduce the number of pediatric injuries from firearms. She described gun violence prevention as directly related to her work as a pediatrician.<ref name= "Brack2026" /> She announced her candidacy for [[South Carolina's 1st congressional district]] in November 2021; it was her first time running for political office.<ref name="RenaudBrams2021">{{Cite news |date= November 8, 2021 |last1=Renaud |first1=Tim |last2= Brams |first2=Sophie |title= Local pediatrician, Dr. Annie Andrews, to run for South Carolina's First Congressional District |url= https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/local-pediatrician-dr-annie-andrews-to-run-for-south-carolinas-first-congressional-district/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221026205445/https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/local-pediatrician-dr-annie-andrews-to-run-for-south-carolinas-first-congressional-district/ |archive-date= October 26, 2022 |access-date= May 24, 2026 |work= [[WCBD-TV]] |language= en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last= Spence |first= Sam |date= November 8, 2021 |title= Mace gets Democratic challenger from MUSC pediatrician Annie Andrews |url= https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2021/11/08/mace-gets-democratic-challenger-from-musc-pediatrician-annie-andrews/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |work= Charleston City Paper |language= en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329002502/https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2021/11/08/mace-gets-democratic-challenger-from-musc-pediatrician-annie-andrews/|archive-date=March 29, 2023}}</ref> She won the Democratic nomination in June 2022 after running unopposed.<ref>{{Cite news |date= June 13, 2022 |title= SC Primary: Who is on the ballot? |url= https://www.wyff4.com/article/south-carolina-primary-who-is-on-ballot/40272622 |access-date= May 24, 2026 |website= [[WYFF]] |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221020033130/https://www.wyff4.com/article/south-carolina-primary-who-is-on-ballot/40272622 |archive-date= October 20, 2022 |url-status= live}}</ref>

In September, the incumbent Republican Nancy Mace began running campaign ads accusing Andrews of engaging in "child abuse" by providing [[gender-affirming care]] and surgery to minors. In a statement, Andrews responded: "I knew politics would be ugly but I never thought I'd see the day when my own congresswoman would accuse me—a pediatrician for over a decade—of 'child abuse' […] I do not support gender-affirming surgery for anyone under 18—nor does my hospital perform those procedures. What I support is evidence-based medical care, with parental consent, for teens struggling with gender identity issues […] Her vicious and disgusting attacks against me and LGBTQ kids puts them at an increased risk of targeted attacks and self-harm behaviors."<ref>{{Cite news |last= Sophie |first= Brams |date= September 28, 2022 |title= Annie Andrews takes leave from MUSC amid political attacks |url= https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/annie-andrews-responds-to-political-attacks/ |work= [[WCBD]] |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260112104931/https://www.counton2.com/news/your-local-election-hq/annie-andrews-responds-to-political-attacks/ |archive-date= January 12, 2026 |access-date= September 1, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> Andrews also said that because of the ads, she would begin taking unpaid leave from her job as a pediatrician at [[MUSC Children's Hospital]] and would increase her personal security while she considered legal action, citing threats made against doctors earlier that year, including a bomb threat against the [[Boston Children's Hospital]].<ref>{{Cite news |last= Dress |first= Brad |date= September 28, 2022 |title= Democratic candidate slams GOP's Nancy Mace for 'bigoted attacks' |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3666011-dem-candidate-slams-gops-nancy-mace-for-bigoted-attacks/ |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20241208123404/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3666011-dem-candidate-slams-gops-nancy-mace-for-bigoted-attacks/ |archive-date= December 8, 2024 |work= [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |language= en-US|url-status= live}}</ref>

In October, Andrews was endorsed by the [[editorial board]] of the ''[[Charleston City Paper]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=October 19, 2022 |title=Our View: Elect Dr. Annie Andrews to Congress |url=https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2022/10/19/our-view-elect-dr-annie-andrews-to-congress/ |access-date=May 24, 2026 |website=Charleston City Paper |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260731174537/https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2022/10/19/our-view-elect-dr-annie-andrews-to-congress/ |archive-date=July 31, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrews lost the general election on November 8, receiving 42.5% of the vote to Mace's 56.5%. She called Mace to concede soon afterward, and Mace responded by thanking her for "stepping into the arena". Andrews said she was unsure where her political career would go next, but that her candidacy had encouraged more medical professionals to follow in her footsteps by running for elected office.<ref>{{Cite news |date= November 8, 2022 |title= South Carolina First Congressional District Election Results |url= https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-south-carolina-us-house-district-1.html |access-date= May 24, 2026 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250119110319/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-south-carolina-us-house-district-1.html |archive-date= January 19, 2025 |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Byrd |first= Caitlin |date= November 8, 2022 |title= Republican US Rep. Nancy Mace defeats Democrat Annie Andrews |url= https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/election2022/republican-us-rep-nancy-mace-defeats-democrat-annie-andrews/article_6eef3fa6-59ff-11ed-a902-07a68160a59d.html |access-date= May 24, 2026 |website= Post and Courier |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260524165339/https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/election2022/republican-us-rep-nancy-mace-defeats-democrat-annie-andrews/article_6eef3fa6-59ff-11ed-a902-07a68160a59d.html |archive-date= May 24, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref>

In April 2023, Andrews announced the creation of a PAC called "Their Future. Our Vote".<ref name= ":2">{{Cite web |last= Altimari |first= Daniela |date= April 17, 2023 |title= Pediatrician launches new PAC focused on children |url= https://rollcall.com/2023/04/17/pediatrician-launches-new-pac-focused-on-children/ |access-date= July 14, 2026 |website= Roll Call |language= en-US |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260727182557/https://rollcall.com/2023/04/17/pediatrician-launches-new-pac-focused-on-children/ |archive-date= July 27, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Byrd |first= Caitlin |date= April 17, 2023 |title= SC Democrat Annie Andrews launches PAC to elect candidates who will advocate for children |url= https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/sc-democrat-annie-andrews-launches-pac-to-elect-candidates-who-will-advocate-for-children/article_9fea39bc-da10-11ed-9d3d-2369fee91c70.html |access-date= May 24, 2026 |website= [[Post and Courier]] |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260731164651/https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/sc-democrat-annie-andrews-launches-pac-to-elect-candidates-who-will-advocate-for-children/article_9fea39bc-da10-11ed-9d3d-2369fee91c70.html |archive-date= July 31, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref>

==U.S. Senate==
===Elections===
====2026====
{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
[[File:Annie Andrews Senate Campaign Building.jpg|thumb|Andrews's field office in Columbia, South Carolina|upright]]On May 29, 2025, Andrews announced her candidacy for the United States Senate in 2026, aiming to unseat incumbent Republican senator [[Lindsey Graham]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chornobroff |first=Shaun |date=May 29, 2025 |title=Former MUSC pediatrician announces campaign to oust Lindsey Graham |url=https://scdailygazette.com/2025/05/29/former-musc-pediatrician-announces-campaign-to-oust-lindsey-graham/ |access-date=May 24, 2026 |website=SC Daily Gazette |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260731174538/https://scdailygazette.com/2025/05/29/former-musc-pediatrician-announces-campaign-to-oust-lindsey-graham/ |archive-date=July 31, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kinnard |first=Meg |date=May 29, 2025 |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham gets a 2026 challenge from Democrat Annie Andrews, who ran against Nancy Mace |url=https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-26ae12355e9e5d1a44047bcbb6cdc36c |access-date=May 24, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608121209/https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-26ae12355e9e5d1a44047bcbb6cdc36c |archive-date=June 8, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> She said she decided to run for Senate after President [[Donald Trump]] appointed [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr.]] as [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services|Secretary of Health]].<ref>{{Cite news |last= Mazzei |first= Patricia |date= July 13, 2026 |title= Lindsey Graham's Death Transforms the Race for His Democratic Challenger |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/13/us/politics/annie-andrews-south-carolina-senate.html |access-date= July 14, 2026 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref>

Andrews was endorsed by former [[Democratic National Committee]] chair [[Jaime Harrison]], who ran against Graham [[2020 United States Senate election in South Carolina|in 2020]].<ref>{{Cite web |date= June 4, 2025 |title= Jaime Harrison Endorses Dr. Annie Andrews For U.S. Senate |url= https://theminorityeye.com/jaime-harrison-endorses-dr-annie-andrews-for-u-s-senate/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |language= en-US|website= The MinorityEye}}</ref> She was also endorsed by the [[314 Action]] Fund,<ref>{{cite press release |last= |date= June 18, 2025 |title= 314 Action Fund Endorses Dr. Annie Andrews for U.S. Senate, as a Guardian of Public Health |url= https://314action.org/2025/06/18/314-action-fund-endorses-dr-annie-andrews-for-u-s-senate-as-a-guardian-of-public-health/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |publisher= 3.14 Action}}</ref> [[EMILYs List]],<ref>{{cite press release |title= EMILYs List Endorses Dr. Annie Andrews in South Carolina's U.S. Senate Race |url= https://emilyslist.org/news/emilys-list-endorses-dr-annie-andrews-in-south-carolinas-u-s-senate-race/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |publisher= EMILYs List |language= en-US |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260610072619/https://emilyslist.org/news/emilys-list-endorses-dr-annie-andrews-in-south-carolinas-u-s-senate-race/ |archive-date= June 10, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> the [[Joint Action Committee for Political Affairs|JAC]], and [[Vote Mama]].<ref>{{cite press release |title= Vote Mama PAC {{!}} Candidates |url= https://www.votemamapac.org/candidates |access-date= May 24, 2026 |publisher= Vote Mama PAC |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240116182045/https://www.votemamapac.org/candidatesold |archive-date= January 16, 2024 |url-status= live}}</ref>

On June 9, 2026, Andrews won the Democratic nomination, and was expected to face Graham.<ref>{{Cite news |date= June 10, 2026 |title= Dr. Annie Andrews wins Democratic nomination for U.S Senate |url= https://abcnews4.com/news/local/dr-annie-andrews-wins-democratic-nomination-for-us-senate |access-date= June 15, 2026 |work= [[WCIV]] |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260610014918/https://abcnews4.com/news/local/dr-annie-andrews-wins-democratic-nomination-for-us-senate |archive-date= June 10, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date= June 9, 2026 |title= Democrat Annie Andrews to face off against Sen. Lindsey Graham in South Carolina, CBS News projects |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-lindsey-graham-democratic-primary-results/ |access-date= August 26, 2026 |website= CBS News |language= en-US |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260826071105/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-lindsey-graham-democratic-primary-results/ |archive-date= August 26, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> On July 11, Graham died,<ref>{{Cite web |last= Reagan |first= Nick |date= September 26, 2025 |title= Meet the candidate: Dr. Annie Andrews (for US Senate) |url= https://www.live5news.com/2025/09/26/we-palmetto-meet-candidate-dr-annie-andrews-us-senate/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |website= www.live5news.com |language= en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260714141657/https://www.live5news.com/2025/09/26/we-palmetto-meet-candidate-dr-annie-andrews-us-senate/ |archive-date= July 14, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> and two days later, his sister, [[Darline Graham|Darline]], was appointed to serve the remainder of his term.<ref name= "ShabadCoronell_Uribe2026-1">{{Cite web |date= July 13, 2026 |title= South Carolina governor appoints Lindsey Graham's sister to finish his Senate term |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/trump-lindsey-graham-sister-darline-mcmaster-appoint-senate-term-rcna587302 |access-date= July 13, 2026 |publisher= NBC News |language= en |first1= Rebecca |last1= Shabad |first2= Raquel |last2= Coronell Uribe}}</ref> On August 25, Darline Graham won the [[South Carolina Republican Party]] nomination in a special primary runoff.<ref>{{Cite news |date= August 26, 2026 |title= Trump Senate Pick Darline Graham Wins GOP Runoff in South Carolina |url= https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-senate-pick-darline-graham-wins-gop-runoff-295b08aa |access-date= August 26, 2026 |work= [[The Wall Street Journal]] |last1= Davis |first1= Maya |last2= McWhirter |first2= Cameron |last3= LeVine |first3= Marianne |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260826034116/https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-senate-pick-darline-graham-wins-gop-runoff-295b08aa |archive-date= August 26, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref> Andrews faced Graham,  [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] nominee Mark Hackett, [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] Kasie Whitener, and write-in candidate [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]] in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-19 |title=United States Senate election in South Carolina, 2026 |url=https://ballotpedia.org/United_States_Senate_election_in_South_Carolina,_2026 |access-date=2026-11-04 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref>

On November 3, Andrews won the general election,<ref name="Hubbard2026-1">{{cite news |last=Hubbard |first=Kaia |date=November 4, 2026| title=Annie Andrews wins South Carolina 2026 Senate election, flipping seat for Democrats |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-2026-senate-annie-andrews-darline-graham/ |publisher= [[CBS News]] |access-date= November 4, 2026}}</ref> becoming the first woman to be elected to the Senate from South Carolina.<ref name="GrisalesSchapitl2026-1">{{cite news |last1= Grisales |first1= Claudia |last2= Schapitl |first2= Lexie |date= November 3, 2026| title= Democrat Annie Andrews wins South Carolina Senate race in major upset |url= https://www.npr.org/2026/11/03/g-s1-151666/south-carolina-senate-race-andrews-graham |publisher= [[NPR]] |access-date=November 4, 2026}}</ref> The seat had been in Republican hands without interruption since 1964, when [[Strom Thurmond]] switched parties. Thurmond retired in 2003 and handed the seat to Lindsey Graham, who held it until his death.

== Political positions ==
Andrews describes herself as a pragmatic, "[[radical centrist]]".<ref name="POLITICO2026-1">{{Cite web |date=2026-11-04 |title=‘They’re Being Steamrolled’: Why South Carolina Is Sending a Democrat to the Senate |url=https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2026/11/04/south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-interview-01148925 |access-date=2026-11-10 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref> Andrews supports abortion rights and [[Universal background check|universal background checks]] while differing with some of the Democratic Party's positions: she supports strengthened border security, [[Term limits in the United States|term limits]], a ban on congressional stock trading, and Trump's "[[no tax on tips]]" and "no tax on [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]]" proposals.<ref name="POLITICO2026-1" />

=== Healthcare affordability and Medicare ===
In an October 28, 2026, interview, Andrews said:<ref>{{Cite web |publisher = WIS News 10 |date= 2026-10-28 |title= FULL INTERVIEW: Annie Andrews shares priorities for Senate ahead of Election Day |url= https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-annie-andrews-shares-priorities-senate-ahead-election-day/ |access-date= 2026-10-30 |website= [[WISTV]] |language= en}}</ref>

I know as a pediatrician that prevention is actually a cost-effective approach to building a health care system. So the more folks who have access to primary care doctors for early diagnosis and treatment of diseases, the less our entire health care system is going to cost. My plan includes a public option, and a public option doesn't mean the government is paying for someone's health care. A public option means that you or I have the option to purchase an affordable public plan if we choose.

In an August 2026 interview on [[NewsNation]], Andrews said she considered [[Single-payer healthcare|Medicare for All]] (single-payer healthcare) "a policy from a few decades ago". She explained, "I'm a pragmatic person. When you work as a physician, you have to be a problem solver. You roll up your sleeves, you solve the problem in front of you, you work with whoever you need to work with, you don't let perfect be the enemy of good". <ref>{{Cite news |title= South Carolina Senate Democratic nominee: 'Medicare for All is a policy from a few decades ago' |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/6054250-medicare-all-annie-andrews-south-carolina-senate/ |work= [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref>

Her priorities on the issue include reversing government healthcare cuts and increasing prescription drug affordability. These measures include, reversing the Medicaid cuts from the [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act]]{{efn|During a 2026 campaign stop in Myrtle Beach, she said, "My number one priority is to stop the Medicaid budget cuts, because it'll be devastating to access to health care across the state. In South Carolina, rural life, those will close and also have a ripple effect on everyone in the health care system. And insurance premiums have already, in some cases, doubled and tripled..."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Payne |first1=Livi |title=US Senate candidate Dr. Annie Andrews discusses health care, rising costs at Myrtle Beach town hall |url=https://www.wbtw.com/news/grand-strand/myrtle-beach/us-senate-candidate-annie-andrews-discusses-health-care-rising-costs-at-myrtle-beach-town-hal}}</ref>}} restoring the enhanced [[Affordable Care Act tax provisions]], and increasing prescription drug price negotiation under Medicare.<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Dayen |first=David |date=2026-10-08 |title=South Carolina Senate Hopeful Runs on Health Care Populism |url=https://prospect.org/2026/10/08/south-carolina-senate-hopeful-runs-on-health-care-populism-annie-andrews/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=[[The American Prospect]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013230053/https://prospect.org/2026/10/08/south-carolina-senate-hopeful-runs-on-health-care-populism-annie-andrews/ |archive-date=October 13, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Reproductive healthcare and access ===
Andrews campaigned on a women's right to choose and increasing access to health services. On her campaign website she said, "These decisions belong to women and families, not to politicians". She vowed to restore the protections of Roe v. Wade, guarantee access to contraception and infertility care, and expand maternal health services, especially in rural communities. Finally, she mentions, "No woman should have to leave her state or risk her life to get medical care".<ref>{{Cite web |title= Reproductive Freedom: Trust Women |url=https://drannieandrews.com/issue/reproductive-freedom-trust-women/ |access-date= 2026-11-05 |website= Dr. Annie Andrews for Senate |language= en-US}}</ref>

=== LGBTQ+ rights ===
In an October 5, 2026, [[Truth Social]] post, Trump used [[Anti-LGBTQ rhetoric|anti-LGBT slurs]] against Andrews, calling her "Annie [[Tranny|Trannie]]". Andrews is not transgender.<ref name="al">{{Cite news |date= 2026-10-06 |title=Trump faces bipartisan outrage after nicknaming South Carolina Democrat with anti-trans slur |url=https://www.al.com/politics/2026/10/trump-faces-bipartisan-outrage-after-nicknaming-south-carolina-democrat-with-anti-trans-slur.html |access-date=2026-10-29 |work=al.com |language=en}}</ref> The post was widely condemned.<ref name="al" /><ref>{{Cite web |date= October 5, 2026 |title= Trump uses anti-trans slur against Democratic Senate candidate in South Carolina |website= [[NBC News]] |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/trump-anti-trans-slur-annie-andrews-south-carolina-democrat-senate-rcna601767 |access-date= October 6, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Several days after Trump's post, Andrews released an ad saying that she opposed "biological men playing women's sports" and would not allow "gender surgery for minors", contradicting a 2022 [[X (social network)|X]] post denouncing the Save Women's Sports Act, a bill barring transgender athletes from girls' and women's sports teams in public schools, as "shameful", saying she would "always support trans youth".<ref>{{Cite news |date= October 9, 2026 |title= Annie Andrews answers Trump's transphobic slur with ad rejecting trans-inclusivity |url= https://www.thepinknews.com/2026/10/09/annie-andrews-trump-slur/ |access-date= October 9, 2026 |work= [[PinkNews]] |last= Lee-Zogbessou |first= Jasmine |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20261009122456/https://www.thepinknews.com/2026/10/09/annie-andrews-trump-slur/ |archive-date= October 9, 2026 |url-status= live}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
In 2011, Andrews married Charlie Andrews, a [[Neurology|neurological]] critical care doctor at MUSC. They have three children.<ref name="Brack2026">{{Cite web |last= Brack |first= Andy |date= July 13, 2022 |title= Andrews embraces the science of decision-making |url= https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2022/07/13/andrews-embraces-the-science-of-decision-making/ |access-date= May 24, 2026 |website= [[Charleston City Paper]] |language= en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230205225047/https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2022/07/13/andrews-embraces-the-science-of-decision-making/|archive-date=February 5, 2023}}</ref> Andrews filed for divorce in August 2023, and it was finalized in 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Valeski |first=Lucy |date=October 19, 2026 |title=How much did Senate candidate Annie Andrews pay in taxes? Here's what returns show |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article317553990.html |access-date=November 3, 2026 |website=The State}}</ref>
